{
  "_comment": "Aelgard drop tables — Marstead itemization layer. This is the design bible for drops, NOT the runtime tables (those live in src/content/aelgard/monsters-mega.js and src/atoms/definitions/drop-tables.js, which the engine reads at boot). This JSON encodes: (a) the PHILOSOPHY — unique rare drops, reagent pairs, encounter-specific BiS — so the codex/narrator/slayer-master have a single source of truth; (b) the REAGENT GRAPH — which new-boss drops upgrade which old items, keeping old content alive (Marstead pillar 3); (c) encounter-specific BiS monsters (~10, like Arclight vs demons in OSRS).",
  "_version": "1.0.0",
  "_philosophy": {
    "pillar_4_non_degenerate": "Every rare drop from monster X must be UNIQUE to X, or serve as a REAGENT that combines with other items to upgrade them. No 'generic rare sword' shared across 10 monsters. The item graph must be navigable as a design object.",
    "pillar_3_old_content_alive": "A new boss's reagent drop pairs with an OLD item to produce the upgrade. You cannot skip low-tier content by grinding high-tier bosses; the upgrade requires both. This is the 'reagent system.'",
    "pillar_12_encounter_specific_BiS": "~10 monsters carry slayer-niche drops — weapons that are BiS only against that monster's tag. This prevents a single universal weapon from dominating PvE.",
    "rarity_distribution_target": "~10% always / ~40% common / ~30% uncommon / ~15% rare / ~5% very_rare per monster. Collection-log uniques are independent rolls with 16-4096 denominator.",
    "drop_weight_convention": "weights sum to a clear denominator. 'Nothing' filler is explicit. Always drops have no weight (they are guaranteed)."
  },
